  <article class="prose">
    <h1>27 Biweekly &amp; 53 Weekly Paychecks in 2027</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> 2027 is not a leap year and January 1, 2027 is a Friday. A biweekly schedule has <strong>27 paydays</strong> (and a weekly schedule has <strong>53 paydays</strong>) in 2027 <em>only</em> if your first 2027 payday is Friday, January 1, 2027.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-back">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/" style="text-decoration: none;">Check 27 vs. 26 Paychecks in the Calculator →</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Why the January 1 Holiday Matters (Constructive Receipt)</h2>
    <p>
      Under the federal constructive receipt rule (<strong>26 CFR 1.451-2</strong>), wages and 401(k) deferrals count in the tax year they are <em>actually paid</em>, not the year the hours were worked.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>If your employer pays on <strong>January 1, 2027</strong>, that paycheck is 2027 income—giving you 27 biweekly paydays (or 53 weekly paydays), ending December 31, 2027.</li>
      <li>Because January 1 is New Year’s Day (a federal and bank holiday), many employers shift that payday one day earlier to <strong>December 31, 2026</strong>. If paid on December 31, 2026, it counts toward your 2026 limit, leaving you with 26 biweekly (or 52 weekly) paydays in 2027.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2027 Max-Out Amounts: 26 vs. 27 Biweekly Paychecks</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Under 50 ($25,500):</strong> $980.76* (26 paychecks) vs. <strong>$944.44</strong> (27 paychecks)</li>
      <li><strong>50–59 &amp; 64+ ($34,000):</strong> $1,307.69 (26 paychecks) vs. <strong>$1,259.25*</strong> (27 paychecks)</li>
      <li><strong>Ages 60–63 ($37,250):</strong> $1,432.69 (26 paychecks) vs. <strong>$1,379.62*</strong> (27 paychecks)</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

